bottom quartile among Basic Materials peers. Each employee generates $7K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $1K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 15\u00d7 revenue per employee (reasonable relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags moderate compensation governance risk (6/10).","financial_summary":["Worthington Steel, Inc. is a small-cap basic materials business with a $1.9B market cap on $3.4B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +0% over the past three years, which puts it in mature, slow-growth territory. Operating margins are contracting by -2.9pp, the first warning that competitive pressure is eroding the franchise.","Returns on capital run at 10% against a 11% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests, and 13% gross margins are typical of commodity economics where competitors can replicate the product cheaply.","The business throws off $80M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 2 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 23.67\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, debt is moderate at 2.1\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, serviceable in normal conditions but worth watching through a downturn, and thin -0.0\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield is only 1.7%, a thin return of capital, and insiders hold 36% of the shares, so management thinks like owners because it is one \u2014 the strongest alignment there is, and profit capture is only 0.0\u00d7 the fair share, suggesting no structural edge inside its industry."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Price sits 119% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left, and the cushion against estimate error has been bid away (confirmed by 2 other valuation measures)","sev":"red","cat":"valuation","magnitude":1.1938162962557273},{"text":"Piotroski score 3/9 \u2014 the fundamentals are deteriorating on multiple fronts","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Payout ratio of 376% leaves essentially no coverage if earnings wobble","sev":"red","cat":"dividend"},{"text":"Operating margins softening -2.9pp YoY \u2014 early warning on the moat","sev":"amber","cat":"trend"},{"text":"Operating margins 9% below sector median \u2014 a cost disadvantage the moat cannot hide","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Altman Z in the grey zone \u2014 financial health warrants monitoring","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Above-average short interest at 7.1% of float","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Trade policy and tariff whiplash \u2014 materials producers are first-order casualties of protectionist swings, and margin gets squeezed from both input and output pricing.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Minimal goodwill on the balance sheet \u2014 low impairment risk"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"Head to Head Survey: Worthington Steel (NYSE:WS) & Alamos Gold (NYSE:AGI)","source":"thelincolnianonline.com","link":"https://www.thelincolnianonline.com/2026/11/13/head-to-head-survey-worthington-steel-nysews-alamos-gold-nyseagi.html","date":"2026-11-13","timestamp":1794589130.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.924,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"159,781 Shares of Worthington Steel, Inc. $WS Bought by Lido Advisors LLC","source":"thelincolnianonline.com","link":"https://www.thelincolnianonline.com/2026/11/13/159781-shares-of-worthington-steel-inc-ws-bought-by-lido-advisors-llc.html","date":"2026-11-13","timestamp":1794565146.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.863,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Comparing Worthington S